exports[`envelope and child brief the courier-orchestrate skill 2`] = `
"How to split a task across OpenCode sessions with opencode-courier, and bring the results back up. Your brief, or the opencode-courier role line in your system prompt, names your depth in the session tree and its limits. If courier_spawn is not among your tools, you cannot split: do the task yourself, and report upward as your brief says if a session started you.

1. Decide: split or do.
   - At the root, first call courier_recall once: it lists how earlier trees in this project were split and how each part went, so weigh that; one call, before you decide, never in a loop. Below the root, read none: the records describe whole trees, and the root weighed them before it split.
   - Split when the task has 2 or more independent, substantial parts touching separate files or areas; do it yourself when it is small, sequential or tightly coupled. A part that is itself big and divisible is split again by the session that gets it. Several whole jobs in one prompt, such as 4 tickets, are that many parts: each session runs this playbook for its job, stages, review and all, so the tree below you repeats its shape once per job.
   - What you read and write yourself stays in your context for every later turn, so keep the deciding, checking and integrating, and hand off the long reading and writing.
   - Only the root, the session nobody started, may start exactly one session for the whole task, and only when the task is heavy (long reading, many edits, long runs) and its own context is worth keeping. It still verifies and integrates that session's report itself (steps 5 to 7), and does a small task itself.
   - Every other session never starts exactly one session for the whole task: it only adds a hop, and a chain of single hops uses up the tree's depth.
   - Say which in one line of your reply, before you start, so that a reasoned decision to do it yourself can be told from one never made: split: no, because …; considered: <the parts you weighed> or split: <n> parts: … (one phrase per part); the root has a third form, split: 1 part: <the task>, to keep my context.
   Parts a task can have, besides its edits, each one session where it is worth a context of its own:
   - a scout: a session with isolate: false reads the code, or a library's or a host's source, tries things and reports what it found, before you plan;
   - the implementers, isolate: true where two edit at once, each with its checks and what to report;
   - its e2e scenario, its documentation, sometimes a second test file;
   - a review stage, after the merge, when the changes are done and it is to see the whole: one review session with fresh context, which makes no fixes of its own. Put in its task the diff to read: when you are isolated, your own courier/<session> branch, committed first, against the commit your worktree started from; else your checkout's HEAD, with the merges committed, against the commit you started from, which you note before your first spawn. It may hand the reading to 2 or more reviewers (one group, each reads that diff from one angle and reports its findings), then starts one verifier with their reports: a fresh session that checks each finding against the diff, drops what does not hold, and starts one implementor for what holds, or 2 or more when the fixes are independent, isolate: true each then, merging their branches after (step 7); an implementor fixes what it should and refuses, with the reason, what it should not. Where the code is: when you work in the project's checkout, the review session, the verifier and a lone implementor go without isolate, and it commits in place; when you are isolated, start each of them with isolate: true, so that each worktree starts from the one above, and each merges the branch reported to it before it reports (step 7); reviewers only read and need no worktree. Each reports to the one that started it; the review session reports to you what was found, what held, what was fixed (the commits) and what was refused and why. It does not replace step 6: running the checks on the fixed tree stays yours, and so does settling a refusal you disagree with. Go through steps 3 to 7 once more for the stage, and do its work yourself when the levels below you are too few for it. One session for one stage, after a stage of 2 or more parts or one you did yourself, is a part of the task, like a staged core, not the whole; a reviewer alone with nothing before it is not a stage.
   When the core is coupled: an implementation task with one tightly coupled core usually still has parts from that list; weigh each of them. When the other parts depend on names and texts the core fixes, split in stages: the core first, yourself when it is small or by one session when it is big, merged and committed before the rest start; then, if 2 or more parts remain, one session per remaining part, in one group. A staged core is one part of the task, not the whole task, so the rule against starting exactly one session for the whole task does not apply to it when 2 or more parts follow it.
2. Plan the parts so that no two edit the same files. Write each task so the session can do it without you: what to produce and where, how to check it, what to report. Say if it may split its part further, and to run its checks through the project's scripts (step 6): each permission request of a session below you wakes you and waits on the person. Name the skills it should load for its part, from those listed to you: it sees the same list, not your choice; one permission rule for skill covers their loads, as one covers the checks. Say in the task when the part is a whole job that delivers itself (step 7), since an isolated session's brief otherwise says its branch is for you to merge.
3. Start them: one courier_spawn per part that runs at once, all with the same group name, so you are woken once with every report; isolate: true for each part that edits files in parallel with another (commit your own work first if you are isolated yourself). When each part runs a review stage of its own, count 6 live sessions per part against maxTotal (a part, its review session and its reviewers are live together at the peak): start the parts that fit in one group, and the rest in a group of their own once the first group has reported. A job that depends on another starts after that one is done, as a stage: merged into your checkout, or, when it was delivered instead (step 7), started with isolate: true and told the delivering session's courier/<session> branch, from its report, to merge into its worktree first. Leave model and variant unset, so a part runs on your own model, unless the part plainly calls for another: a cheaper model or a lower variant for a small, well-specified part (a rename, a lookup), a stronger model or a higher variant for a design question or a hard bug. For long work, courier_later a check-in for yourself, and courier_cancel it when the reports arrive.
4. End your turn: reply without calling more tools. Do not poll with courier_status, courier_children, courier_tree or sleep. The group's reports, a blocked report, a question, a permission request, a failed turn or a silent end each start a new turn for you. So does a session's message without a status, and one from the person or from the session that started you.
   Answering from below: follow the notice. For a permission request, propose once with courier_answer, or reject with the reason when the person has told you to refuse it or it plainly goes against what they asked; the person decides, in OpenCode's prompt in your session. For a question, ask the person with your question tool, with the questions the notice gives; their answer goes on by itself. When the notice says OpenCode already shows it to the person, call nothing unless they give you their answer. Never answer for them.
   Passing a message on: when it changes what other parts need (an assumption of the split that is wrong, a shared file or API that behaves otherwise, a decision of the person's), pass it on at once with courier_send, only to the sessions it affects, before they go further, then end your turn again; if it breaks the split, courier_stop the parts it breaks and plan them again, or send them a corrected task. If a session started you and its other parts need it too, send it up to that session as well, without a status. Progress that changes nothing for the others goes to no one.
5. Read each report by its status and its artifacts (branch, commits, files, checks). done: verify it. partial: what is left and why; have the session finish it with a courier_send, or do it yourself. blocked: make the decision it asks for and send it with courier_send; if only the person can make it, ask them with your question tool and pass the answer on. failed: read the error; message the session to try again, start a replacement, or carry on without it. A review stage's report lists what was found, held, fixed and refused: read the refusals, fix or settle yourself any you disagree with, and run the checks on the fixed tree.
6. Verify first-hand: open the files, run the checks. Never hand the checking to another session: a review stage verifies and fixes below you, and running the checks on what it changed is still yours. Run checks through the project's scripts (bun run <script>, npm run <script>), not ./node_modules/.bin/<tool>, so that one permission rule covers them: your own permission prompts are relayed nowhere, and each one stalls the whole tree.
7. Integrate: merge each reported branch into your own checkout (git merge courier/<session>), resolve conflicts yourself, run the checks once more, then courier_cleanup the isolated sessions you are done with. When the parts are whole jobs with a deliverable each (a ticket with its own branch or pull request), the session delivers it and reports the link in its message, still naming its courier/<session> branch when it is isolated; you verify each delivery and merge nothing of it into your checkout.
8. Report upward if a session started you: one courier_send to it, with one status for the whole (done only when every part is verified and integrated, or verified and delivered; partial, blocked or failed otherwise) and the artifacts. At the top, before you reply to the person: if there is something the next tree in this project should know about running a tree like this one (how it was split, what a part got wrong and what caught it, what a brief should have said, what the person decided), one courier_note with that one line; none when there is nothing. Name the job it comes from and the reason, so that a lesson for one kind of job is not taken for every job; the tool refuses a line over its limit rather than clipping it, so write it again shorter. A rule that holds every time goes to AGENTS.md or the skill, through the person, not into a note. Then reply to the person.

When a part goes wrong:
- A failed turn (failed="...") or a silent end (ended="without-report", quoting the session's last reply): message the session with courier_send to carry on, to report, or to try again; or start a replacement. Until it reports, it counts toward the limits.
- A refusal from courier_spawn naming maxDepth, maxChildren or maxTotal: do that part yourself, or end your turn and start it once a report has arrived. Do not retry it in a loop.
- Lost track, after a compaction or a restart: one courier_tree shows every session under you with its depth, its parent and whether it still owes a report; one look, not a loop.
- A subtree that went wrong or is no longer needed: courier_stop its top session; everything under it is stopped too, and nothing in it reports back. cleanup: true removes their worktrees."
`;

